WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.366 --> 00:00:03.254
- The essence of entrepreneurship
is about making meaning.

00:00:03.254 --> 00:00:04.751
This mission statement is going to be good

00:00:04.751 --> 00:00:07.812
for the shareholders, for the
employees, for the customers.

00:00:07.812 --> 00:00:09.268
If HR is there, it's going to be good

00:00:09.268 --> 00:00:10.991
for the whales and the dolphins.

00:00:10.991 --> 00:00:12.274
We're going to take care of everything.

00:00:12.274 --> 00:00:14.618
You should not be afraid
of polarizing people.

00:00:14.618 --> 00:00:15.541
I'm not saying you should go

00:00:15.541 --> 00:00:17.331
and intentionally piss them off.

00:00:17.331 --> 00:00:18.841
Using your rock star roommate

00:00:18.841 --> 00:00:21.342
who happened to be with you
the weekend you got drunk

00:00:21.342 --> 00:00:23.630
and decided to start
this pet food company.

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:25.672
It's not the crappy logo
that your sister-in-law

00:00:25.672 --> 00:00:26.960
designed for free.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.708
You need to tell a story.

00:00:28.708 --> 00:00:30.977
Why did you start eBay?

00:00:30.977 --> 00:00:32.620
Why did you start Google?

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:35.858
And, you know the Latin saying
of, I think, therefore I am?

00:00:35.858 --> 00:00:38.943
For an entrepreneur, it is
I pitch, therefore I am.

00:00:38.943 --> 00:00:40.758
Don't scale too fast.

00:00:40.758 --> 00:00:43.382
I have never seen a company die

00:00:43.382 --> 00:00:46.662
because it could not scale too fast.

00:00:46.662 --> 00:00:48.284
For you to reach critical
mass with a product

00:00:48.284 --> 00:00:50.744
it's not because the Wall Street Journal

00:00:50.744 --> 00:00:52.998
or the New York Times
blessed your product.

00:00:52.998 --> 00:00:54.998
It's because lonelyboy15

00:00:56.676 --> 00:00:58.113
embraced your product.

00:00:58.113 --> 00:00:59.713
At this point in my life,

00:00:59.713 --> 00:01:01.554
you may have noticed, I
really don't give a (bleep)

00:01:01.554 --> 00:01:02.575
what people think.

00:01:02.575 --> 00:01:05.301
- He's a Silicon Valley
marketing executive.

00:01:05.301 --> 00:01:06.754
He was one of the Apple employees

00:01:06.754 --> 00:01:08.613
originally responsible for marketing

00:01:08.613 --> 00:01:11.406
their Macintosh computer in 1984.

00:01:11.406 --> 00:01:15.272
In 2015, he joined the
Wikimedia Board of Trustees.

00:01:15.272 --> 00:01:16.565
He's Guy Kawasaki,

00:01:16.565 --> 00:01:18.605
and here are his Top 10 Rules for Success.

00:01:18.605 --> 00:01:20.938
(whooshing)

00:01:21.951 --> 00:01:23.537
- The first thing I
figured out and learned,

00:01:23.537 --> 00:01:25.214
sometimes the hard way,

00:01:25.214 --> 00:01:27.380
about entrepreneurship is that the core,

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:30.690
the essence of entrepreneurship
is about making meaning.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:33.076
Many, many people start
companies to make money.

00:01:33.076 --> 00:01:35.399
The quick flip, the dot com phenomenon.

00:01:35.399 --> 00:01:37.959
And I have noticed in both the
companies that I've started,

00:01:37.959 --> 00:01:39.898
and funded, and been associated with,

00:01:39.898 --> 00:01:43.758
that those companies that
are fundamentally founded

00:01:43.758 --> 00:01:47.232
to change the world, to make
the world a better place,

00:01:47.232 --> 00:01:49.921
to make meaning, are the
companies that make a difference.

00:01:49.921 --> 00:01:52.037
They are the companies to succeed.

00:01:52.037 --> 00:01:56.173
My naive and romantic belief
is that if you make meaning,

00:01:56.173 --> 00:01:57.946
you'll probably make money.

00:01:57.946 --> 00:01:59.638
But if you set out to make money,

00:01:59.638 --> 00:02:04.621
you will probably not make
meaning and you won't make money.

00:02:04.621 --> 00:02:07.057
So my first thought is,
you need to make meaning.

00:02:07.057 --> 00:02:09.999
That should be the core of
why you start a company.

00:02:09.999 --> 00:02:11.941
There are three ways to make meaning.

00:02:11.941 --> 00:02:14.349
First is to increase the quality of life.

00:02:14.349 --> 00:02:17.179
My background is a Macintosh
division of Apple Computer

00:02:17.179 --> 00:02:20.297
and I can tell you with
total certainty that we were

00:02:20.297 --> 00:02:22.239
not motivated by making money.

00:02:22.239 --> 00:02:24.523
We were motivated by changing
the world to make people

00:02:24.523 --> 00:02:26.082
more creative and more productive.

00:02:26.082 --> 00:02:28.438
We were trying to increase
the quality of life

00:02:28.438 --> 00:02:29.848
for the Macintosh user.

00:02:29.848 --> 00:02:31.570
And that was a great motivation.

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:34.602
It kept us going through
many, many difficult periods.

00:02:34.602 --> 00:02:37.230
We were waking up in the
morning thinking how we could

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:39.376
change people's lives.

00:02:39.376 --> 00:02:43.717
A second way to make
meaning is to right a wrong.

00:02:43.717 --> 00:02:46.983
You know, this fish is going
to die after jumping out

00:02:46.983 --> 00:02:49.406
but to right a wrong means
that you find something

00:02:49.406 --> 00:02:51.044
that's wrong in the world
or you notice something

00:02:51.044 --> 00:02:53.441
that's wrong in the world
and you want to fix that.

00:02:53.441 --> 00:02:56.039
This might be particularly
applicable to not-for-profits

00:02:56.039 --> 00:02:59.466
where, you know, there's
pollution, or there's crime,

00:02:59.466 --> 00:03:01.202
or there's abuse and they just,

00:03:01.202 --> 00:03:04.399
their very core is to end that wrong.

00:03:04.399 --> 00:03:05.570
And the third way to make meaning

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:08.280
is to prevent the end of something good.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.098
That you see something
beautiful, something wonderful

00:03:11.098 --> 00:03:13.660
and you just can't stand the
fact that it's been eroded,

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.292
it's being changed, it's being ruined.

00:03:16.292 --> 00:03:18.726
So I ask you, as you start your companies,

00:03:18.726 --> 00:03:21.295
your not-for-profits, your
churches, your schools,

00:03:21.295 --> 00:03:23.536
whatever you're starting,

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:25.366
please have one of
those three motivations,

00:03:25.366 --> 00:03:26.729
one or more of those motivations.

00:03:26.729 --> 00:03:28.999
If you don't have any
of those motivations,

00:03:28.999 --> 00:03:31.981
I suggest that you
rethink what you're doing.

00:03:31.981 --> 00:03:34.493
Because there are too
many MBAs in the world,

00:03:34.493 --> 00:03:37.792
and I have an MBA, because
there are too many MBAs

00:03:37.792 --> 00:03:41.140
and too many of you have
worked for Bain or McKinsey,

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:44.144
or Arthur D. Little, somehow
you think that you should

00:03:44.144 --> 00:03:46.714
make a mission statement.

00:03:46.714 --> 00:03:49.693
And the way you make a mission
statement is typically this.

00:03:49.693 --> 00:03:53.860
You go on a two-day offsite
with the executive team.

00:03:54.851 --> 00:03:58.887
The executive team, the
first day of this offsite,

00:03:58.887 --> 00:04:01.979
you're outdoors, you're at the
Ritz-Carlton Half Moon Bay,

00:04:01.979 --> 00:04:06.562
for example, because that's
why you go on these offsites.

00:04:06.562 --> 00:04:08.218
So you go to the
Ritz-Carlton Half Moon Bay

00:04:08.218 --> 00:04:10.354
and there's a facilitator
and the first day you create

00:04:10.354 --> 00:04:13.857
these cross functional teams
and the facilitator says,

00:04:13.857 --> 00:04:16.970
alright, one of you from
each team come to the front,

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:20.553
everybody else get behind
him or her, turn around,

00:04:20.553 --> 00:04:23.578
shut your eyes, open up
your arms and fall backwards

00:04:23.578 --> 00:04:27.154
into their arms because
we're going to build trust.

00:04:27.154 --> 00:04:28.026
(audience laughing)

00:04:28.026 --> 00:04:30.276
You're going to learn that your
teammates are there for you.

00:04:30.276 --> 00:04:32.841
So turn around, shut your
eyes, spread your arms,

00:04:32.841 --> 00:04:34.905
and fall backwards.

00:04:34.905 --> 00:04:36.419
So after one day of this bullshit,

00:04:36.419 --> 00:04:39.524
the second day you're all in a small room,

00:04:39.524 --> 00:04:41.606
there's a bunch of white papers, you know,

00:04:41.606 --> 00:04:44.438
those tablets of papers
and there's a facilitator

00:04:44.438 --> 00:04:47.205
who knows nothing about your industry.

00:04:47.205 --> 00:04:48.708
And that facilitator's going to help you

00:04:48.708 --> 00:04:50.255
craft this mission statement.

00:04:50.255 --> 00:04:51.898
And this mission statement
is going to be good

00:04:51.898 --> 00:04:54.991
for the shareholders, for the
employees, for the customers,

00:04:54.991 --> 00:04:57.113
if HR is there it's going
to be good for the whales

00:04:57.113 --> 00:04:59.130
and the dolphins, we're going
to take care of everything.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:00.350
(audience laughing)

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:03.379
And what happens is you get a
mission statement like this.

00:05:03.379 --> 00:05:05.799
The mission of Wendy's is
to deliver superior quality

00:05:05.799 --> 00:05:08.893
products and services for
our customers and communities

00:05:08.893 --> 00:05:11.119
through leadership,
innovation, and partnerships.

00:05:11.119 --> 00:05:12.873
(audience laughing loudly)

00:05:12.873 --> 00:05:13.708
Now,

00:05:13.708 --> 00:05:16.467
(audience applauding)

00:05:16.467 --> 00:05:19.855
don't get me wrong, I love Wendy's.

00:05:19.855 --> 00:05:21.029
I have four children.

00:05:21.029 --> 00:05:24.029
By definition, I like Wendy's, okay?

00:05:25.367 --> 00:05:27.452
But I have to tell you, in
all the times that I've eaten

00:05:27.452 --> 00:05:30.451
at Wendy's it has never
occurred to me that I am

00:05:30.451 --> 00:05:33.308
participating in something
involving leadership,

00:05:33.308 --> 00:05:35.040
innovation, and partnerships.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.931
(audience laughing)

00:05:36.931 --> 00:05:40.633
I dare say that if you ask
Trixie or Biff working at Wendy's

00:05:40.633 --> 00:05:42.290
what is the mission statement of Wendy's,

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:45.063
not one could repeat this.

00:05:45.063 --> 00:05:47.855
I also dare say that not
even the founder of Wendy's,

00:05:47.855 --> 00:05:50.878
Dave Thomas, could repeat this.

00:05:50.878 --> 00:05:52.776
I know that's not true because he's dead.

00:05:52.776 --> 00:05:55.241
(audience laughing)

00:05:55.241 --> 00:05:58.107
This is a useless kind of statement.

00:05:58.107 --> 00:06:01.516
It's too long, it's not
unique, it's not memorable.

00:06:01.516 --> 00:06:05.359
As an entrepreneur, someday
when you have 15,000 employees,

00:06:05.359 --> 00:06:08.151
you're trading on Nasdaq and
you spend two million dollars

00:06:08.151 --> 00:06:11.157
to build your annual report,
fine, make a dumb ass

00:06:11.157 --> 00:06:13.362
mission statement like this.

00:06:13.362 --> 00:06:16.241
But right now, in the
entrepreneurial stage,

00:06:16.241 --> 00:06:19.204
I submit to you, you
should create a mantra.

00:06:19.204 --> 00:06:20.762
Three or four words.

00:06:20.762 --> 00:06:21.994
A mantra.

00:06:21.994 --> 00:06:24.853
Ironically, entrepreneurs
who should have this bias

00:06:24.853 --> 00:06:27.627
toward action often can't get going.

00:06:27.627 --> 00:06:30.605
They are victims of
engineering creeping elegance

00:06:30.605 --> 00:06:33.064
where they always want
to add one more feature.

00:06:33.064 --> 00:06:35.777
Or they're marketing people and they need

00:06:35.777 --> 00:06:37.791
that external validation,

00:06:37.791 --> 00:06:39.426
they want to prove that there's a market,

00:06:39.426 --> 00:06:42.069
they have to read one
more consulting study,

00:06:42.069 --> 00:06:44.234
or they're trying to get
a focus group to prove

00:06:44.234 --> 00:06:45.144
that the product works.

00:06:45.144 --> 00:06:47.468
They need one more focus
group, one more test suite,

00:06:47.468 --> 00:06:49.329
one more something.

00:06:49.329 --> 00:06:50.528
It's a bunch of crap.

00:06:50.528 --> 00:06:53.193
So here's the three recommendations
about getting going.

00:06:53.193 --> 00:06:55.552
The first is you've got to think different

00:06:55.552 --> 00:06:56.933
as an entrepreneur.

00:06:56.933 --> 00:06:59.473
Your goal should not be to
do something 10%, or 15%,

00:06:59.473 --> 00:07:04.160
or 20% better, it should be to
do something 10 times better.

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:06.386
It should not be a
slightly better Apple II,

00:07:06.386 --> 00:07:08.684
it should not be slightly better MS DOS,

00:07:08.684 --> 00:07:11.993
it should be a completely
different operating system.

00:07:11.993 --> 00:07:13.214
Think different.

00:07:13.214 --> 00:07:16.696
Build the product or
service that you love.

00:07:16.696 --> 00:07:19.940
I moderated a panel on Saturday
where a venture capitalist

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:22.156
from Sequoia was on the panel.

00:07:22.156 --> 00:07:27.065
And he said that the best
source of entrepreneurial talent

00:07:27.065 --> 00:07:30.948
for Sequoia, the best
places and companies to fund

00:07:30.948 --> 00:07:33.365
are people under 30 years old

00:07:34.211 --> 00:07:37.075
who are solving a personal problem.

00:07:37.075 --> 00:07:40.407
Hence, Google, hence, Yahoo, hence Sysco.

00:07:40.407 --> 00:07:43.723
That's the kind of sweet spot
for a venture capitalist.

00:07:43.723 --> 00:07:45.188
Think different.

00:07:45.188 --> 00:07:48.524
Don't just do the 15th
thing, think different.

00:07:48.524 --> 00:07:51.498
The second point about getting
going is do not be afraid

00:07:51.498 --> 00:07:53.921
of polarizing people.

00:07:53.921 --> 00:07:55.960
You know, many people are
afraid to polarize people,

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:59.251
and my God, some people
might not like what we do.

00:07:59.251 --> 00:08:00.871
And so they try to create
this product or service

00:08:00.871 --> 00:08:02.070
that everybody likes.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:04.224
That is impossible.

00:08:04.224 --> 00:08:06.755
You should not be afraid
of polarizing people.

00:08:06.755 --> 00:08:08.206
I'm not saying you should
go and intentionally

00:08:08.206 --> 00:08:11.125
piss them off, but you
should not be afraid

00:08:11.125 --> 00:08:12.144
of polarizing people.

00:08:12.144 --> 00:08:15.282
The ideal situation is where
some people really love

00:08:15.282 --> 00:08:18.673
your product and some
people hate your product,

00:08:18.673 --> 00:08:21.842
but very few people feel
sort of disinterested

00:08:21.842 --> 00:08:24.746
or neutral towards your product.

00:08:24.746 --> 00:08:28.532
You know, something
like a Toyota Scion XB,

00:08:28.532 --> 00:08:31.977
you either love that design
and you think it's very cool

00:08:31.977 --> 00:08:34.506
or you think that's a
refrigerator on wheels.

00:08:34.506 --> 00:08:35.573
(audience laughing loudly)

00:08:35.573 --> 00:08:37.426
But there's nothing in middle,

00:08:37.426 --> 00:08:38.292
there's nothing in the middle.

00:08:38.292 --> 00:08:41.352
Same thing with the mini Cooper,
same thing with the Murano,

00:08:41.352 --> 00:08:42.813
same thing with a Macintosh,

00:08:42.813 --> 00:08:44.795
same thing with a Harley Davidson.

00:08:44.795 --> 00:08:47.002
You either love the product
or hate the product,

00:08:47.002 --> 00:08:49.450
but don't be afraid to polarize people.

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:53.141
And the third point is you
need to find a few soul mates.

00:08:53.141 --> 00:08:57.240
I think there's a vast
myth about entrepreneurship

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:00.426
that it's Steve Jobs changing
the world single-handedly,

00:09:00.426 --> 00:09:02.728
Bill Gates changing the
world single-handedly,

00:09:02.728 --> 00:09:05.230
Richard Branson changing
the world single-handedly,

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:07.458
Anita Roddick changing
the world single-handedly,

00:09:07.458 --> 00:09:09.550
Mary Kay changing the
world single-handedly.

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:11.730
It's not true, it's not true.

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.022
You need soul mates, you
need, I think ideally,

00:09:14.022 --> 00:09:16.278
three people in a founding team.

00:09:16.278 --> 00:09:18.680
Because when one person
falls down you need the other

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:20.446
two people to pick you up.

00:09:20.446 --> 00:09:23.015
And when one person is
cold you need other people

00:09:23.015 --> 00:09:24.920
to hug you and keep you warm.

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:26.823
It's very hard to keep yourself warm,

00:09:26.823 --> 00:09:29.342
it's very hard to hug yourself, alright?

00:09:29.342 --> 00:09:30.943
So find a soul mate.

00:09:30.943 --> 00:09:33.104
For Bill Gates there was Steve Ballmer,

00:09:33.104 --> 00:09:35.577
for Steve Jobs there was Steve Wozniak.

00:09:35.577 --> 00:09:38.189
Find a few soul mates
that balance you off,

00:09:38.189 --> 00:09:40.365
that keep you warm, that'll pick you up.

00:09:40.365 --> 00:09:43.831
Mistake number one that
entrepreneurs do is all the time,

00:09:43.831 --> 00:09:46.658
and if you're going to pitch me,
you better avoid this mistake,

00:09:46.658 --> 00:09:49.582
which is to take this
big number and multiply

00:09:49.582 --> 00:09:51.417
by a mere one percent.

00:09:51.417 --> 00:09:53.099
So this is how the pitch goes.

00:09:53.099 --> 00:09:56.326
Pretend you go back in time 15 years ago

00:09:56.326 --> 00:09:58.686
and you're pitching Pets.com.

00:09:58.686 --> 00:10:03.412
So Pets.com was a dot com
start-up, it sold dog food online.

00:10:03.412 --> 00:10:06.232
Okay, so this is the pitch.

00:10:06.232 --> 00:10:09.071
There are 300 million
Americans, these Americans,

00:10:09.071 --> 00:10:12.988
roughly one in four own
a dog, 75 million dogs.

00:10:14.846 --> 00:10:18.079
Each dogs eats two can
of dog food per day,

00:10:18.079 --> 00:10:21.162
150 million cans of dog food per day.

00:10:22.371 --> 00:10:24.812
And this is not B2B,

00:10:24.812 --> 00:10:26.438
this is B2C,

00:10:26.438 --> 00:10:28.498
or more accurately, B2D,

00:10:28.498 --> 00:10:29.488
(audience laughing loudly)

00:10:29.488 --> 00:10:33.405
because dogs, dogs do
not take the weekend off.

00:10:34.686 --> 00:10:36.769
Dogs eat everyday, right?

00:10:37.868 --> 00:10:41.708
So total adjustable market
is one and half million

00:10:41.708 --> 00:10:44.365
cans of dog food per day, or excuse me,

00:10:44.365 --> 00:10:48.282
150 million cans of dog
food per day times 365.

00:10:49.186 --> 00:10:52.131
Now, let's be conservative
because that's a favorite word

00:10:52.131 --> 00:10:54.472
of entrepreneurs, let's be conservative.

00:10:54.472 --> 00:10:57.720
Let's pretend that we can
get a mere one percent

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:00.888
of this market, you know, how
hard could that possibly be

00:11:00.888 --> 00:11:02.881
to get a mere one percent

00:11:02.881 --> 00:11:06.392
of 150 million cans of dog food per day?

00:11:06.392 --> 00:11:09.128
So one percent of 150 million
cans of dog food per day,

00:11:09.128 --> 00:11:10.612
you don't need to go to
Stanford to figure out that,

00:11:10.612 --> 00:11:12.816
it's one and a half million
cans of dog food per day.

00:11:12.816 --> 00:11:14.520
One and half million
cans of dog food per day,

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:16.913
let's say you conservatively
make a buck per can.

00:11:16.913 --> 00:11:18.655
That's a million and a
half dollars per day.

00:11:18.655 --> 00:11:20.753
One and half million dollars
a day of gross revenue

00:11:20.753 --> 00:11:23.914
times 365 equals $500
million dollar business.

00:11:23.914 --> 00:11:27.166
Conservatively speaking, worst case, okay?

00:11:27.166 --> 00:11:30.810
(audience laughing)

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:32.204
Do you not hear this kind of pitch

00:11:32.204 --> 00:11:34.401
every frickin' day of the year?

00:11:34.401 --> 00:11:35.936
Right, right.

00:11:35.936 --> 00:11:38.125
So, that's what people
do and you know what?

00:11:38.125 --> 00:11:40.759
It's total (bleep), it's total (bleep)

00:11:40.759 --> 00:11:44.524
because you cannot address
that kind of market.

00:11:44.524 --> 00:11:46.692
This is saying, you know,
how hard could it be to get

00:11:46.692 --> 00:11:48.964
one percent of the people
in China to drink my soda?

00:11:48.964 --> 00:11:51.582
Well, that's not so easy to
get one percent of the people

00:11:51.582 --> 00:11:53.307
in China to drink your soda.

00:11:53.307 --> 00:11:55.876
So the solution for this
is that you calculate

00:11:55.876 --> 00:11:57.470
from the bottom up.

00:11:57.470 --> 00:12:00.222
Okay, so if you used the mistaken way,

00:12:00.222 --> 00:12:02.935
you calculate one and
half million cans per day,

00:12:02.935 --> 00:12:04.405
conservatively speaking.

00:12:04.405 --> 00:12:05.884
Let's go from the bottom.

00:12:05.884 --> 00:12:08.681
So from the bottom, using
your rockstar roommate

00:12:08.681 --> 00:12:11.026
who happened to be with you
the weekend you got drunk

00:12:11.026 --> 00:12:13.149
and decided to start
this pet food company,

00:12:13.149 --> 00:12:14.301
(audience laughing)

00:12:14.301 --> 00:12:16.711
and because, you know, you
have no technical skills

00:12:16.711 --> 00:12:20.009
and he has one class in
programming, he's the CTO now, okay?

00:12:20.009 --> 00:12:23.061
(audience laughing loudly)

00:12:23.061 --> 00:12:24.653
You know, I can tell you,

00:12:24.653 --> 00:12:26.384
you're familiar with this practice, right?

00:12:26.384 --> 00:12:27.309
Right.

00:12:27.309 --> 00:12:29.712
Okay, so CTO and CEO.

00:12:29.712 --> 00:12:31.958
And so this is how you
do a bottom-up analysis.

00:12:31.958 --> 00:12:35.524
You say, alright, so with
my rockstar, you know, PHP,

00:12:35.524 --> 00:12:39.691
Ruby expert, roommate, he's
going to write this great,

00:12:40.573 --> 00:12:43.723
or she's going to write
this great website and,

00:12:43.723 --> 00:12:46.270
but let's just think, like, so website,

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:49.944
the selling pet food online,
eh, let's figure that we can

00:12:49.944 --> 00:12:53.671
get 50,000 unique visitors a month,

00:12:53.671 --> 00:12:55.889
let's be a little conservative, 50,000.

00:12:55.889 --> 00:12:59.958
And of those 50,000 people,
let's say that using the same

00:12:59.958 --> 00:13:03.827
conservative number, one percent
of the people buy dog food.

00:13:03.827 --> 00:13:05.554
They actually buy dog food, right?

00:13:05.554 --> 00:13:08.804
So one percent of 50,000 is 500, right?

00:13:10.028 --> 00:13:10.861
Is that right?

00:13:10.861 --> 00:13:11.694
Yeah.

00:13:11.694 --> 00:13:12.527
So 500.

00:13:12.527 --> 00:13:14.109
So 500 people buy.

00:13:14.109 --> 00:13:17.476
These 500 people, they buy a case.

00:13:17.476 --> 00:13:21.226
So it's 500 times 30,
so now we're at 15,000.

00:13:22.874 --> 00:13:26.270
So, like, okay, so we have
15,000 cans of dog food per day.

00:13:26.270 --> 00:13:28.096
Let's, you know, let's round up a little.

00:13:28.096 --> 00:13:31.929
So maybe we get to 15,
maybe we get to 20,000.

00:13:32.905 --> 00:13:35.940
But 15,000 or 20,000 is a long way

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:38.655
from one and a half million, right?

00:13:38.655 --> 00:13:40.604
So, using the top down analysis,

00:13:40.604 --> 00:13:41.786
you come up with a conservative

00:13:41.786 --> 00:13:43.980
one and half million per day.

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:47.622
Using the more conservative,
you know, bottom-up analysis,

00:13:47.622 --> 00:13:50.022
you come up with 15,000 per day.

00:13:50.022 --> 00:13:51.111
Let me tell you something.

00:13:51.111 --> 00:13:54.330
You are going to be much
closer to 15,000 per day

00:13:54.330 --> 00:13:56.136
than one and a half million.

00:13:56.136 --> 00:13:59.080
And that's how you prevent this mistake.

00:13:59.080 --> 00:14:00.341
You have to be realistic.

00:14:00.341 --> 00:14:03.885
You can't just go from this
big old number and try to

00:14:03.885 --> 00:14:06.530
scrunch it down using
conservative estimates,

00:14:06.530 --> 00:14:08.576
you need to start from the bottom.

00:14:08.576 --> 00:14:12.107
Can you get 50,000 people
to visit your website a day?

00:14:12.107 --> 00:14:15.426
Will one percent actually
buy the dog food?

00:14:15.426 --> 00:14:17.556
That's a much more relevant way.

00:14:17.556 --> 00:14:20.210
So that's how you avoid this mistake.

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:22.710
And the results will always be

00:14:23.882 --> 00:14:26.645
closer to the bottom-up
analysis than the top down.

00:14:26.645 --> 00:14:29.288
In all my years of working
with entrepreneurs,

00:14:29.288 --> 00:14:33.679
I have never seen a company
meet their financial projection.

00:14:33.679 --> 00:14:34.828
Never.

00:14:34.828 --> 00:14:36.566
It never happens.

00:14:36.566 --> 00:14:39.216
The fifth thing is to weave a MAT.

00:14:39.216 --> 00:14:42.288
MAT stands for milestones,
assumptions, and tasks.

00:14:42.288 --> 00:14:44.676
I happen to love ice hockey.

00:14:44.676 --> 00:14:47.307
I happen to love ice hockey
and there are two of my friends

00:14:47.307 --> 00:14:51.554
here from Ice Oasis where I
play ice hockey in Redwood City.

00:14:51.554 --> 00:14:55.660
And one of the pleasures
of ice hockey is to be

00:14:55.660 --> 00:14:58.103
the first person on the
ice after the Zamboni.

00:14:58.103 --> 00:15:00.569
Because after the Zamboni
has shaved the ice,

00:15:00.569 --> 00:15:04.364
the ice is just pure smooth
virgin ice, no cuts, no ruts.

00:15:04.364 --> 00:15:06.696
It's a beautiful thing and
when you skate you can see

00:15:06.696 --> 00:15:07.820
exactly where you skated.

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:09.534
It's a lovely feeling.

00:15:09.534 --> 00:15:12.036
It's like being the first
person down the mountain

00:15:12.036 --> 00:15:14.086
in skiing or snowboarding.

00:15:14.086 --> 00:15:16.098
A new company is largely like that.

00:15:16.098 --> 00:15:20.491
It's a pure sheet of
wonderful, smooth ice.

00:15:20.491 --> 00:15:23.077
There's no pain in the ass
customers who are trying

00:15:23.077 --> 00:15:25.185
to get a free upgrade,

00:15:25.185 --> 00:15:28.162
there's no stupid employees
you made a mistake hiring,

00:15:28.162 --> 00:15:30.390
there's not the crappy
commercial real estate

00:15:30.390 --> 00:15:32.752
that you paid $15 bucks
a month for in the height

00:15:32.752 --> 00:15:34.365
of the dot com phenomena,

00:15:34.365 --> 00:15:35.740
there's not the crappy furniture

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:37.764
you bought at Office Depot one day,

00:15:37.764 --> 00:15:40.264
it's pure, pure wonderful ice.

00:15:41.751 --> 00:15:42.942
It's not the crappy logo

00:15:42.942 --> 00:15:45.050
that your sister-in-law designed for free.

00:15:45.050 --> 00:15:46.448
(audience laughing)

00:15:46.448 --> 00:15:48.849
You're starting from scratch,
it's beautiful, beautiful.

00:15:48.849 --> 00:15:50.998
The Zamboni just left the ice.

00:15:50.998 --> 00:15:52.894
When you have that situation,

00:15:52.894 --> 00:15:55.360
it's very difficult to prioritize.

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:57.344
This is about prioritization.

00:15:57.344 --> 00:15:58.763
The first thing you need to do

00:15:58.763 --> 00:16:02.520
is come up with a handful of milestones.

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:06.121
Milestones are things
like finishing the design,

00:16:06.121 --> 00:16:08.097
shipping the software.

00:16:08.097 --> 00:16:10.728
When you're starting a company,
you often think, my God,

00:16:10.728 --> 00:16:12.932
we could either finish the design today

00:16:12.932 --> 00:16:15.475
or go order stationary.

00:16:15.475 --> 00:16:19.195
Seems like they're both
about the same importance.

00:16:19.195 --> 00:16:20.354
It's not true.

00:16:20.354 --> 00:16:22.953
A milestone is something that
you would call up your spouse

00:16:22.953 --> 00:16:26.391
and say, honey, today we shipped.

00:16:26.391 --> 00:16:28.777
That's very different than
calling up your spouse

00:16:28.777 --> 00:16:32.744
and saying, honey, today
we ordered stationary.

00:16:32.744 --> 00:16:33.954
(audience laughing)

00:16:33.954 --> 00:16:36.386
That's not quite the same.

00:16:36.386 --> 00:16:37.646
The next thing you do is you write down

00:16:37.646 --> 00:16:39.452
the assumptions of your company.

00:16:39.452 --> 00:16:41.560
How many sales calls can you make per day?

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:43.419
What's the customer ROI?

00:16:43.419 --> 00:16:46.930
How much does it cost to
install our software or product?

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:48.965
These assumptions change
your business model.

00:16:48.965 --> 00:16:50.376
Very few companies or start-ups

00:16:50.376 --> 00:16:52.492
write them down and test them.

00:16:52.492 --> 00:16:54.540
Write them down and test them.

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:56.975
And the third thing is then to do tasks.

00:16:56.975 --> 00:16:59.817
Tasks are the things that
help you either accomplish

00:16:59.817 --> 00:17:02.205
a milestone or test an assumption.

00:17:02.205 --> 00:17:05.804
But the priority for an
entrepreneur is milestones,

00:17:05.804 --> 00:17:09.017
assumptions, and tasks, M-A-T.

00:17:09.017 --> 00:17:11.100
You need to tell a story.

00:17:12.310 --> 00:17:15.239
Most people, particularly in technology,

00:17:15.239 --> 00:17:17.256
are horrible at telling stories.

00:17:17.256 --> 00:17:19.369
You need to tell a story.

00:17:19.369 --> 00:17:21.454
Why did you start eBay?

00:17:21.454 --> 00:17:22.963
Why did you start Google?

00:17:22.963 --> 00:17:24.342
Why did you start Apple?

00:17:24.342 --> 00:17:27.849
You can even make up the
story after the fact, okay?

00:17:27.849 --> 00:17:28.824
(audience laughing)

00:17:28.824 --> 00:17:33.083
One of the great legends of
Silicon Valley is that eBay

00:17:33.083 --> 00:17:35.291
was because Pierre Omidyar's girlfriend

00:17:35.291 --> 00:17:38.291
wanted to sell PEZ dispensers, okay?

00:17:39.295 --> 00:17:41.371
The truth is that he
really wanted to create

00:17:41.371 --> 00:17:46.136
a perfect market where demand
and supply cross each other.

00:17:46.136 --> 00:17:47.361
The perfect market.

00:17:47.361 --> 00:17:48.736
And to have a perfect market,

00:17:48.736 --> 00:17:51.175
you need to eliminate geography.

00:17:51.175 --> 00:17:54.512
The story of the PEZ
dispenser is after the fact.

00:17:54.512 --> 00:17:57.271
Now, I'm not encouraging you to lie,

00:17:57.271 --> 00:18:00.758
but I am telling you a
story is much more powerful

00:18:00.758 --> 00:18:02.361
when you launch.

00:18:02.361 --> 00:18:05.543
Why you two in a garage
knew there was a better way

00:18:05.543 --> 00:18:08.722
to search or you wanted to
bring personal computers

00:18:08.722 --> 00:18:12.301
to more people, or you wanted
a place where people could

00:18:12.301 --> 00:18:16.976
upload video of people dropping
Mentos into diet Cokes,

00:18:16.976 --> 00:18:17.809
you know,

00:18:17.809 --> 00:18:18.642
(audience laughing)

00:18:18.642 --> 00:18:20.746
whatever it is, tell a story.

00:18:20.746 --> 00:18:24.161
The next thing is the art of pitching.

00:18:24.161 --> 00:18:27.273
Pitching is usually used to raise money.

00:18:27.273 --> 00:18:29.095
But pitching, more generically,

00:18:29.095 --> 00:18:31.894
is about the process of seeking agreement.

00:18:31.894 --> 00:18:34.382
You can seek agreement for money,

00:18:34.382 --> 00:18:36.342
you can seek agreement to recruit,

00:18:36.342 --> 00:18:38.124
you can see agreement for partnership,

00:18:38.124 --> 00:18:40.403
and you can seek agreement for sales.

00:18:40.403 --> 00:18:41.586
Pitching.

00:18:41.586 --> 00:18:44.993
You know the Latin saying
of I think, therefore I am?

00:18:44.993 --> 00:18:48.358
For an entrepreneur it is
I pitch, therefore I am.

00:18:48.358 --> 00:18:51.414
An entrepreneur is always pitching.

00:18:51.414 --> 00:18:54.311
There is what I call the 10/20/30 rule.

00:18:54.311 --> 00:18:57.052
The 10 part of the 10/20/30
rule is that the optimal

00:18:57.052 --> 00:19:00.742
number of slides for a
PowerPoint pitch is 10.

00:19:00.742 --> 00:19:05.127
Maybe it's 15, but it's not
the 50 that you think it is.

00:19:05.127 --> 00:19:07.851
If you can cover these
10 subjects very well,

00:19:07.851 --> 00:19:10.755
then next step will be due diligence.

00:19:10.755 --> 00:19:14.499
Many people think that if
you put 50 slides together

00:19:14.499 --> 00:19:17.224
the audience will be
overcome with shock and awe

00:19:17.224 --> 00:19:21.002
and all they can ask is how
do I wire you the money?

00:19:21.002 --> 00:19:23.931
I have never ever seen
that happen, alright?

00:19:23.931 --> 00:19:26.435
The purpose of a pitch is
to get to the next stage

00:19:26.435 --> 00:19:27.665
of due diligence.

00:19:27.665 --> 00:19:31.242
The purpose of a pitch
is not to try to close

00:19:31.242 --> 00:19:34.436
the venture capital firm,
the partner, the customer,

00:19:34.436 --> 00:19:35.974
in that meeting.

00:19:35.974 --> 00:19:38.528
The second point is the 20.

00:19:38.528 --> 00:19:39.361
I think you should be able to

00:19:39.361 --> 00:19:41.480
use these 10 slides in 20 minutes.

00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:43.166
You may have a 60 minute meeting

00:19:43.166 --> 00:19:45.685
but I guarantee you it'll start late.

00:19:45.685 --> 00:19:47.673
The partners from the
firm will come in late.

00:19:47.673 --> 00:19:49.198
You're using a Windows laptop,

00:19:49.198 --> 00:19:50.768
you can't make it work with the projector.

00:19:50.768 --> 00:19:51.601
(audience laughing)

00:19:51.601 --> 00:19:53.273
Something will go wrong.

00:19:53.273 --> 00:19:55.533
Something will go wrong.

00:19:55.533 --> 00:19:58.616
And the third point is 30 point font.

00:20:00.869 --> 00:20:05.386
The smallest font you should
use on a slide is 30 points.

00:20:05.386 --> 00:20:07.256
This single recommendation
will prevent you

00:20:07.256 --> 00:20:09.530
from getting in trouble with most slides.

00:20:09.530 --> 00:20:13.003
Lots of people like to use
eight, 10, or 12 point font,

00:20:13.003 --> 00:20:15.127
they like to write out
the entire paragraph

00:20:15.127 --> 00:20:17.306
and then they read the paragraph.

00:20:17.306 --> 00:20:20.454
People can read faster than you can talk

00:20:20.454 --> 00:20:23.111
so if you put all of the
text that you want to say

00:20:23.111 --> 00:20:26.069
up on the slide, they will
read it, if they can see it,

00:20:26.069 --> 00:20:29.454
and they will be done
before you've explained it.

00:20:29.454 --> 00:20:31.875
The ideal size font is 30.

00:20:31.875 --> 00:20:33.903
If you think this is too dogmatic,

00:20:33.903 --> 00:20:36.080
let me give you an algorithm.

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:39.048
The algorithm is find out
who the oldest person will be

00:20:39.048 --> 00:20:42.094
in the meeting, take that
age and divide by two.

00:20:42.094 --> 00:20:43.028
(audience chuckling)

00:20:43.028 --> 00:20:43.861
Okay?

00:20:43.861 --> 00:20:46.027
So unless you're
presenting to 16 year olds,

00:20:46.027 --> 00:20:48.656
do not use the eight point font.

00:20:48.656 --> 00:20:49.969
You scale too fast.

00:20:49.969 --> 00:20:53.601
This is, the foundation of this mistake

00:20:53.601 --> 00:20:54.706
is the previous slide.

00:20:54.706 --> 00:20:56.706
So this is how scaling too fast works.

00:20:56.706 --> 00:21:00.007
So, we have now calculated
that conservatively speaking

00:21:00.007 --> 00:21:01.075
we're going to do one and a half million

00:21:01.075 --> 00:21:02.250
cans of dog food per day.

00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:03.122
Oh, my God.

00:21:03.122 --> 00:21:05.432
We need to really put in
a service infrastructure.

00:21:05.432 --> 00:21:07.735
We need to have multiple
warehouses because we need

00:21:07.735 --> 00:21:09.045
the dog food shipping out.

00:21:09.045 --> 00:21:10.929
A million and a half
cans of dog food per day,

00:21:10.929 --> 00:21:12.848
you know, we should have a headquarter,

00:21:12.848 --> 00:21:15.053
we should have a shipping
facility in northern California,

00:21:15.053 --> 00:21:17.026
southern California, we need one in Texas,

00:21:17.026 --> 00:21:18.808
we need one in Chicago,
we need one in Boston,

00:21:18.808 --> 00:21:19.866
we need one in Miami,

00:21:19.866 --> 00:21:21.741
we need one in Burlington, North Carolina.

00:21:21.741 --> 00:21:23.466
We need one everywhere because, damn it,

00:21:23.466 --> 00:21:25.050
we're going to have great service.

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:27.489
And so we need these multiple warehouses,

00:21:27.489 --> 00:21:30.264
we need support 24 x 7
because people are going to be

00:21:30.264 --> 00:21:32.469
buying dog food all over
time, all the places,

00:21:32.469 --> 00:21:34.605
one and a half million cans a
day, conservatively speaking.

00:21:34.605 --> 00:21:36.103
(audience chuckling)

00:21:36.103 --> 00:21:37.159
Ahh!

00:21:37.159 --> 00:21:38.044
And so what happens?

00:21:38.044 --> 00:21:42.194
You make your burn come
out, half a million dollars.

00:21:42.194 --> 00:21:43.769
And guess what?

00:21:43.769 --> 00:21:47.994
Your rockstar CTO roommate
is late on the software.

00:21:47.994 --> 00:21:49.717
And so you're burning
half a million dollars

00:21:49.717 --> 00:21:51.727
because you have all this
infrastructure, you have people,

00:21:51.727 --> 00:21:54.408
you have buildings, you
have all these systems

00:21:54.408 --> 00:21:58.782
and all these things in place
and the software is late.

00:21:58.782 --> 00:22:01.769
This goes on for a year, right?

00:22:01.769 --> 00:22:05.422
And so during that year
your investors are now

00:22:05.422 --> 00:22:07.835
getting a little antsy but
you say to your investors,

00:22:07.835 --> 00:22:11.740
you know, we've invested so
much training these people

00:22:11.740 --> 00:22:15.980
how to sell and support dog
food and these are rockstar

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:19.194
support people and our CTO says that

00:22:19.194 --> 00:22:22.789
we're going to ship next month
so it would be really stupid

00:22:22.789 --> 00:22:26.172
to fire these rockstar support people

00:22:26.172 --> 00:22:28.743
because in 30 days we're
going to have to hire back

00:22:28.743 --> 00:22:31.000
the same kind of people and
then we'll have to train them

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:34.166
because it's so hard to
sell dog food online,

00:22:34.166 --> 00:22:35.961
we have so much invested in them,

00:22:35.961 --> 00:22:38.142
so let's keep these people on.

00:22:38.142 --> 00:22:40.620
And for the first three or
four board meetings your board,

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:42.814
because they're mostly
looking at their Blackberries,

00:22:42.814 --> 00:22:44.883
are not going to even hear this
and they're going to sort of

00:22:44.883 --> 00:22:46.754
nod to do anything, alright?

00:22:46.754 --> 00:22:49.431
But by about the six,
seventh, eighth board meeting

00:22:49.431 --> 00:22:51.128
when you're burning
half a million dollars,

00:22:51.128 --> 00:22:53.013
your board is going to wake
up and they're going to say,

00:22:53.013 --> 00:22:56.757
you know something, we're
starting to not believe you.

00:22:56.757 --> 00:23:00.424
And so the key here is
don't scale too fast.

00:23:01.387 --> 00:23:04.075
I have never seen a company die

00:23:04.075 --> 00:23:07.509
because it could not scale too fast.

00:23:07.509 --> 00:23:10.330
For once in my life, God
willing, I would like to have

00:23:10.330 --> 00:23:13.185
a problem where a company
I'm affiliated with has

00:23:13.185 --> 00:23:17.197
such demand it cannot
keep up with the demand.

00:23:17.197 --> 00:23:21.916
God, please give me that problem
just once in my life, okay?

00:23:21.916 --> 00:23:25.302
So the solution to this
problem is my philosophy that

00:23:25.302 --> 00:23:27.689
you eat what you kill.

00:23:27.689 --> 00:23:30.173
In other words, you
don't build the kitchen,

00:23:30.173 --> 00:23:32.948
you don't build the chef,
you don't get the waiters,

00:23:32.948 --> 00:23:35.502
you don't get the restaurant,
you don't get anything

00:23:35.502 --> 00:23:37.589
until you actually kill something.

00:23:37.589 --> 00:23:40.199
And then once you kill
something, once you get the sale,

00:23:40.199 --> 00:23:42.780
then you build the infrastructure,

00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:44.943
then you build the support.

00:23:44.943 --> 00:23:46.663
Now, you're going to
say, well, Guy, you know,

00:23:46.663 --> 00:23:50.215
if you do this and
you're actually on time,

00:23:50.215 --> 00:23:52.195
then you might have a bad reputation.

00:23:52.195 --> 00:23:53.738
You can't fulfill orders fast enough,

00:23:53.738 --> 00:23:55.498
you can't do something
like that, but again,

00:23:55.498 --> 00:23:57.556
I've never seen it happen.

00:23:57.556 --> 00:23:59.947
So a very good discipline is, you know,

00:23:59.947 --> 00:24:02.780
if you think that you're going to get

00:24:05.031 --> 00:24:07.600
25 million page views a month,

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:11.178
so you need to have two sets of buildings,

00:24:11.178 --> 00:24:12.983
and you need two sets of IT

00:24:12.983 --> 00:24:15.699
and all of this customer
service, I'll tell you what,

00:24:15.699 --> 00:24:18.081
when you approach 25 million,

00:24:18.081 --> 00:24:20.511
let's just worry about it then, okay?

00:24:20.511 --> 00:24:23.158
But until then, because
odds are, you will be late.

00:24:23.158 --> 00:24:24.936
Odds are you will be late.

00:24:24.936 --> 00:24:26.769
So, eat what you kill.

00:24:27.939 --> 00:24:30.737
You need to plant many seeds.

00:24:30.737 --> 00:24:33.147
This is completely contrary
to classic marketing.

00:24:33.147 --> 00:24:36.525
Classic marketing is
your plant a few seeds

00:24:36.525 --> 00:24:38.111
because you know exactly

00:24:38.111 --> 00:24:40.086
who's going to embrace your product.

00:24:40.086 --> 00:24:44.362
And you water and fertilize
just those seeds, you focus.

00:24:44.362 --> 00:24:46.129
I think that it's completely wrong.

00:24:46.129 --> 00:24:50.231
In the old days, you would
focus on the A-listers

00:24:50.231 --> 00:24:54.847
and the top tier journalists,
i.e. Wall Street Journal,

00:24:54.847 --> 00:24:58.043
New York Times, Business
Week, Fortune, Forbes.

00:24:58.043 --> 00:25:01.050
What you did is you sucked
up to them because you hoped

00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:03.374
that these great oracles
of wisdom and knowledge

00:25:03.374 --> 00:25:06.228
would embrace your product
and tell the hoy-palloy

00:25:06.228 --> 00:25:09.561
and the great unwashed masses
to embrace your product.

00:25:09.561 --> 00:25:11.045
Okay, so you sucked up to them.

00:25:11.045 --> 00:25:13.339
If you didn't have time
to suck up to them,

00:25:13.339 --> 00:25:15.748
you hired a PR firm and
paid them $10,000 bucks

00:25:15.748 --> 00:25:17.245
to suck up for you.

00:25:17.245 --> 00:25:20.458
Then the PR firm would hire
an oriental art history

00:25:20.458 --> 00:25:23.927
from Wellesley to do the
sucking up for you, okay?

00:25:23.927 --> 00:25:25.599
This doesn't work anymore

00:25:25.599 --> 00:25:27.368
because the world has been inverted.

00:25:27.368 --> 00:25:29.182
The world has been inverted.

00:25:29.182 --> 00:25:32.420
And now, for you to reach
critical mass with a product,

00:25:32.420 --> 00:25:34.921
it's not because the Wall Street Journal

00:25:34.921 --> 00:25:37.123
or the New York Times
blessed your product,

00:25:37.123 --> 00:25:41.716
it's because lonelyboy15
embraced your product.

00:25:41.716 --> 00:25:42.684
(audience chuckling)

00:25:42.684 --> 00:25:46.243
And lonelyboy15 told his 20 friends

00:25:46.243 --> 00:25:50.410
who told their 20 friends who
eventually told Tiffany517

00:25:51.599 --> 00:25:54.779
and lonelygirl to also
embrace your product.

00:25:54.779 --> 00:25:57.010
And what happens, and this is how Twitter,

00:25:57.010 --> 00:25:58.714
and this is how Facebook happen,

00:25:58.714 --> 00:26:01.681
it's not because the New York
Times saw Twitter or Facebook

00:26:01.681 --> 00:26:03.896
on the first day they
launched and said, ah ha,

00:26:03.896 --> 00:26:05.717
we have seen the future.

00:26:05.717 --> 00:26:08.296
We know with total
certainty someday Facebook

00:26:08.296 --> 00:26:11.252
will have more members
than every other country

00:26:11.252 --> 00:26:12.888
except China and India.

00:26:12.888 --> 00:26:13.918
(audience chuckling)

00:26:13.918 --> 00:26:16.158
Show me a New York Times,
Business Week, Fortune,

00:26:16.158 --> 00:26:19.151
Forbes article that predicted
the success of Twitter,

00:26:19.151 --> 00:26:21.818
or Facebook, or Apple, or Yahoo.

00:26:23.110 --> 00:26:25.461
Pick any of the success
stories, none of them.

00:26:25.461 --> 00:26:29.155
So what you need to do
is plant many seeds.

00:26:29.155 --> 00:26:31.391
Put your prototype out there.

00:26:31.391 --> 00:26:34.361
Cover the Earth with it
because you do not know

00:26:34.361 --> 00:26:36.778
who your lonelyboy15 will be.

00:26:37.987 --> 00:26:41.820
This is some loser of a
person who has no life

00:26:42.789 --> 00:26:46.956
who's a database administrator
in the bowels of HP, right?

00:26:47.855 --> 00:26:49.767
And this person may be the person

00:26:49.767 --> 00:26:51.660
that makes your product a a success.

00:26:51.660 --> 00:26:55.365
You cannot reach those people
by focusing and using PR firms

00:26:55.365 --> 00:26:59.189
who have hired oriental art
history majors from Wellesley.

00:26:59.189 --> 00:27:01.606
You need to plant many seeds.

00:27:02.487 --> 00:27:05.327
I have much, I have about
1000 times more communication

00:27:05.327 --> 00:27:07.244
