Leave it in the Comments,
I'll join in the discussion.

00:30:58.282 --> 00:30:59.539
Thank you so much for watching.

00:30:59.539 --> 00:31:01.821
Continue to believe and I'll see you soon.

00:31:01.821 --> 00:31:03.904
(whoosh)

00:31:08.570 --> 00:31:12.237
- Partnership is a very,
very overused word.

00:31:13.381 --> 00:31:17.744
In many circumstances,
the word partnership means

00:31:17.744 --> 00:31:19.411
lack of sales, okay?

00:31:20.560 --> 00:31:22.890
So since you don't have
any revenue you say,

00:31:22.890 --> 00:31:26.234
but we have very promising partnerships.

00:31:26.234 --> 00:31:29.264
In other words, it's total bull(bleep).

00:31:29.264 --> 00:31:32.263
Most partnerships are total bull(bleep).

00:31:32.263 --> 00:31:36.430
There's only one thing that
you care about, it's sales.

00:31:37.621 --> 00:31:38.989
Remember, this is the most important thing

00:31:38.989 --> 00:31:43.244
I could tell you tonight,
sales fixes everything.

00:31:43.244 --> 00:31:44.077
(audience chuckling)

00:31:44.077 --> 00:31:44.910
Okay?

00:31:44.910 --> 00:31:47.069
No bullshit partnership,
nothing strategic,

00:31:47.069 --> 00:31:50.191
you're either selling
or you're not selling.

00:31:50.191 --> 00:31:51.751
Partnerships are bull(bleep).

00:31:51.751 --> 00:31:53.771
If you want to apply a
more sophisticated test

00:31:53.771 --> 00:31:57.604
to a partnership, when
you form a partnership,

00:31:58.861 --> 00:32:00.361
did you boot Excel

00:32:02.181 --> 00:32:05.598
and either lower costs or increase sales?

00:32:06.618 --> 00:32:07.935
That's it.

00:32:07.935 --> 00:32:11.479
Because if you form a partnership
and it doesn't force you

00:32:11.479 --> 00:32:15.593
to recalculate your financials,
it's total bull(bleep).

00:32:15.593 --> 00:32:16.426
Okay?

00:32:16.426 --> 00:32:17.298
That's the test.

00:32:17.298 --> 00:32:19.556
It is a spreadsheet test for partnerships.

00:32:19.556 --> 00:32:22.014
Most partnerships you announce something,

00:32:22.014 --> 00:32:24.359
two ego maniac CEOs
who don't know anything

00:32:24.359 --> 00:32:26.651
about the business go
to a press conference,

00:32:26.651 --> 00:32:30.004
you announce this great thing,
and then nothing happens.

00:32:30.004 --> 00:32:31.861
The middles and the bottoms
of your organization

00:32:31.861 --> 00:32:34.670
can't stand each other,
nothing happens, okay?

00:32:34.670 --> 00:32:36.441
So use the spreadsheet test.

00:32:36.441 --> 00:32:38.072
Don't form partnerships.

00:32:38.072 --> 00:32:40.072
Instead, focus on sales.

00:32:41.962 --> 00:32:43.141
Focus on sales.

00:32:43.141 --> 00:32:47.037
I swear to you that most
entrepreneurs use the P word

00:32:47.037 --> 00:32:49.348
in order to blow smoke because

00:32:49.348 --> 00:32:52.181
they cannot use the S word, sales.

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:55.583
The ideal number of times
you should use the word

00:32:55.583 --> 00:32:58.818
partnership in a presentation is one.

00:32:58.818 --> 00:32:59.764
Like just say it once.

00:32:59.764 --> 00:33:01.384
You have one partnership.

00:33:01.384 --> 00:33:04.717
But if you start spitting
it out like it's the key

00:33:04.717 --> 00:33:08.572
strategic strength for
your company, oh, my God,

00:33:08.572 --> 00:33:10.232
you are in deep trouble, okay?

00:33:10.232 --> 00:33:14.048
But generally speaking, unless
it changes your spreadsheet,

00:33:14.048 --> 00:33:15.782
partnerships are bull(bleep).

00:33:15.782 --> 00:33:17.790
Invoke reciprocation.

00:33:17.790 --> 00:33:21.290
This is a a carpet that depicts the battle

00:33:22.231 --> 00:33:26.813
between Italy and Ethiopia
because Italy invaded Ethiopia

00:33:26.813 --> 00:33:27.896
in the 1930s.

00:33:29.411 --> 00:33:33.411
When that invasion occurred
the people of Mexico

00:33:34.677 --> 00:33:38.884
donated money and sent it
to the people of Ethiopia.

00:33:38.884 --> 00:33:43.373
Something like 80 years later,
Mexico had this horrendous

00:33:43.373 --> 00:33:46.144
series of earthquakes,
lots of dead people,

00:33:46.144 --> 00:33:47.992
lots of injury, lots of suffering.

00:33:47.992 --> 00:33:50.988
The people of Ethiopia, even
though they were in a famine,

00:33:50.988 --> 00:33:54.300
collected money and sent money to Mexico

00:33:54.300 --> 00:33:58.467
because they felt the necessary
obligation to reciprocate.

00:33:59.508 --> 00:34:01.591
Fast forward a few years.

00:34:03.749 --> 00:34:07.358
About right after the Civil
War, the people of Charleston,

00:34:07.358 --> 00:34:11.676
South Carolina were using
bucket brigades to fight fires.

00:34:11.676 --> 00:34:14.275
The people of New York,
including Union soldiers,

00:34:14.275 --> 00:34:17.614
heard about this and
they donated money to buy

00:34:17.614 --> 00:34:21.418
the City of Charleston a fire truck.

00:34:21.418 --> 00:34:24.196
That first fire truck
was on a boat that sank.

00:34:24.196 --> 00:34:25.706
So they had to raise money again

00:34:25.706 --> 00:34:28.648
and send another truck to Charleston.

00:34:28.648 --> 00:34:31.809
Excuse me, fire truck to Charleston.

00:34:31.809 --> 00:34:34.158
Fast forward to right after 9/11.

00:34:34.158 --> 00:34:37.406
The people of Charleston raised
over half a million dollars

00:34:37.406 --> 00:34:40.342
to buy a fire truck for
the people of New York

00:34:40.342 --> 00:34:42.957
because the people of Charleston
pledged that if New York

00:34:42.957 --> 00:34:45.489
ever needed its help,
it would come through,

00:34:45.489 --> 00:34:46.822
135 years later.

00:34:48.529 --> 00:34:52.275
Reciprocation is a very powerful force

00:34:52.275 --> 00:34:55.728
to make your enchantment endure.

00:34:55.728 --> 00:34:59.163
It means that you have
to pay it forward though.

00:34:59.163 --> 00:35:00.016
And I will give you

00:35:00.016 --> 00:35:03.518
a couple of power tips
about reciprocation.

00:35:03.518 --> 00:35:07.994
When you pay it forward, when
you do something for somebody,

00:35:07.994 --> 00:35:11.366
if they have any social
skills they will thank you.

00:35:11.366 --> 00:35:12.199
Right?

00:35:12.199 --> 00:35:13.923
They will express gratitude.

00:35:13.923 --> 00:35:18.684
When that occurs, the
optimal thing for you to say

00:35:18.684 --> 00:35:20.601
is not you are welcome.

00:35:21.710 --> 00:35:23.730
This is a lesson from Robert Cialdini.

00:35:23.730 --> 00:35:27.499
The optimal thing for you to
say when someone thanks you

00:35:27.499 --> 00:35:31.666
for doing something is I know
you would do the same for me.

00:35:33.198 --> 00:35:35.997
(audience laughing)

00:35:35.997 --> 00:35:39.243
Because when you say that,
you are doing two things.

00:35:39.243 --> 00:35:43.854
One is, you are saying to
the person, you have class.

00:35:43.854 --> 00:35:47.473
I did something for
you, you're thanking me,

00:35:47.473 --> 00:35:49.285
and I know you would do the same for me

00:35:49.285 --> 00:35:52.037
because you are a classy guy,
you believe in reciprocation.

00:35:52.037 --> 00:35:53.549
That's one thing you're saying.

00:35:53.549 --> 00:35:55.770
The second thing you're saying is,

00:35:55.770 --> 00:35:57.580
let me tell you something, you owe me.

00:35:57.580 --> 00:35:59.616
(audience laughing loudly)

00:35:59.616 --> 00:36:02.278
I'm telling you right now, you owe me.

00:36:02.278 --> 00:36:03.711
Let's just make it clear.

00:36:03.711 --> 00:36:05.515
You frickin' owe me.

00:36:05.515 --> 00:36:07.757
(audience laughing)

00:36:07.757 --> 00:36:09.813
Another power tip.

00:36:09.813 --> 00:36:12.445
You might think that when
someone owes you something,

00:36:12.445 --> 00:36:15.002
you should hesitate on
letting them pay you back.

00:36:15.002 --> 00:36:17.211
You know, I know you
would do the same for me,

00:36:17.211 --> 00:36:18.381
but you don't really have to.

00:36:18.381 --> 00:36:19.944
It's okay, it's okay.

00:36:19.944 --> 00:36:22.238
God bless you, go on with your life.

00:36:22.238 --> 00:36:26.502
The better thing for you to do
is enable him to pay me back.

00:36:26.502 --> 00:36:29.520
Because you don't want him
carrying around this guilt

00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:32.241
and hesitation his whole life, right?

00:36:32.241 --> 00:36:34.699
So what you want to do
is relieve the guilt.

00:36:34.699 --> 00:36:37.949
Okay, you can shine my shoes, you know.

00:36:38.896 --> 00:36:41.453
You can defragment my hard disk.

00:36:41.453 --> 00:36:44.828
You can delete the spam
from my, whatever you want,

00:36:44.828 --> 00:36:48.168
you can make my Windows 7
laptop print, whatever, right?

00:36:48.168 --> 00:36:49.828
(audience laughing)

00:36:49.828 --> 00:36:51.804
So by doing that,

00:36:51.804 --> 00:36:55.101
by doing that you alleviate the guilt

00:36:55.101 --> 00:36:57.934
that he's carrying upon shoulders.

00:36:59.266 --> 00:37:01.637
What you're doing is you
are clearing the deck.

00:37:01.637 --> 00:37:02.822
You're clearing the desk.

00:37:02.822 --> 00:37:03.969
Now we're equal.

00:37:03.969 --> 00:37:06.017
I can do more favors for you.

00:37:06.017 --> 00:37:08.000
You can even do favors for me.

00:37:08.000 --> 00:37:10.397
You're relieving the pressure.

00:37:10.397 --> 00:37:12.647
So let people pay you back.

00:37:14.631 --> 00:37:17.498
It's good for them, it's good for you.

00:37:17.498 --> 00:37:20.765
The next recommendation is
that you hire infected people.

00:37:20.765 --> 00:37:21.765
I think that

00:37:24.521 --> 00:37:28.688
an infected founding team
is probably more important

00:37:29.823 --> 00:37:33.041
than a founding team that on
paper has a good background

00:37:33.041 --> 00:37:36.362
and work experience or education.

00:37:36.362 --> 00:37:40.246
You know, the people who
love the product or service,

00:37:40.246 --> 00:37:42.336
that's more important than their degrees.

00:37:42.336 --> 00:37:44.647
It's more important than
their work experience.

00:37:44.647 --> 00:37:49.193
Focus on the people who
love the product or service.

00:37:49.193 --> 00:37:53.363
This means fundamentally you
ignore what's irrelevant.

00:37:53.363 --> 00:37:54.390
And in many cases,

00:37:54.390 --> 00:37:57.548
education and work
experience is irrelevant.

00:37:57.548 --> 00:38:01.498
This is completely contrary to
what most VCs will tell you.

00:38:01.498 --> 00:38:04.704
If you have VCs further
on into this course

00:38:04.704 --> 00:38:06.983
and if you ask him, well,
what are you looking for,

00:38:06.983 --> 00:38:08.704
they're going to tell you, and
I'll tell you that right now,

00:38:08.704 --> 00:38:09.924
you can skip that lecture.

00:38:09.924 --> 00:38:12.544
They're going to say we want proven teams

00:38:12.544 --> 00:38:15.196
with proven technology
and proven traction.

00:38:15.196 --> 00:38:19.037
In other words, we want
people who have founded Sysco,

00:38:19.037 --> 00:38:21.142
won a Nobel prize,

00:38:21.142 --> 00:38:23.560
and they're doing $50
million dollars in revenue.

00:38:23.560 --> 00:38:26.952
That's an early stage deal
for a VC today, alright?

00:38:26.952 --> 00:38:28.821
By contrast, Garage is
in a different business.

00:38:28.821 --> 00:38:30.751
We want unproven teams, unproven traction,

00:38:30.751 --> 00:38:32.591
and unproven technology.

00:38:32.591 --> 00:38:34.410
It's a completely different world.

00:38:34.410 --> 00:38:36.827
The irrelevant thing is that,

00:38:39.080 --> 00:38:40.522
you know, think about what an entrepreneur

00:38:40.522 --> 00:38:42.009
has to go through.

00:38:42.009 --> 00:38:45.484
So let's take what might
seem very attractive

00:38:45.484 --> 00:38:47.129
to many venture capitalists,

00:38:47.129 --> 00:38:50.470
let's say that on your
founding team is someone

00:38:50.470 --> 00:38:54.226
who was a Senior Vice
President at Microsoft.

00:38:54.226 --> 00:38:56.931
Most VCs would salivate at the thought.

00:38:56.931 --> 00:38:59.666
This is a proven executive.

00:38:59.666 --> 00:39:02.504
This executive has been
with Microsoft for 10 years,

00:39:02.504 --> 00:39:05.302
has helped Microsoft grow, etc., etc.

00:39:05.302 --> 00:39:09.976
But if you peel the layers
of the onion one level lower

00:39:09.976 --> 00:39:12.106
you would see that that executive,

00:39:12.106 --> 00:39:14.303
during his or her career to Microsoft,

00:39:14.303 --> 00:39:16.077
had on average a war chest

00:39:16.077 --> 00:39:19.505
of $50 billion dollars in Microsoft.

00:39:19.505 --> 00:39:23.867
During that time frame they
had a market share percentage

00:39:23.867 --> 00:39:25.534
of roughly 95%.

00:39:26.633 --> 00:39:28.863
During that person's tenure at Microsoft,

00:39:28.863 --> 00:39:31.963
that person's greatest fear
was the Antitrust Division

00:39:31.963 --> 00:39:34.862
of the US Department of Justice.

00:39:34.862 --> 00:39:37.755
Now you're going to take that
proven executive from Microsoft

00:39:37.755 --> 00:39:41.542
and stick them in a garage with $500,000,

00:39:41.542 --> 00:39:44.898
zero brand awareness, zero market share.

00:39:44.898 --> 00:39:47.461
I would make the case
that that might not be

00:39:47.461 --> 00:39:50.316
the ideal hire for you, okay?

00:39:50.316 --> 00:39:51.860
Now you may be wondering why in the world

00:39:51.860 --> 00:39:54.400
are there diamond rings in this picture?

00:39:54.400 --> 00:39:58.567
It is because I am living
proof of this theory.

00:39:58.567 --> 00:40:02.761
I graduated from Stanford in
a psych degree, as I told you,

00:40:02.761 --> 00:40:05.674
I attended UC Davis Law
School, and when you say

00:40:05.674 --> 00:40:07.542
you attended a school it
means you didn't finish.

00:40:07.542 --> 00:40:09.284
(audience laughing)

00:40:09.284 --> 00:40:12.125
I attended two weeks so
I really didn't, in fact,

00:40:12.125 --> 00:40:14.191
I never got started actually.

00:40:14.191 --> 00:40:18.274
And after that, I went
into the jewelry business.

00:40:19.579 --> 00:40:22.467
I enrolled the following year
in an MBA program at UCLA

00:40:22.467 --> 00:40:23.792
and while I was there I started working

00:40:23.792 --> 00:40:24.720
in the jewelry business.

00:40:24.720 --> 00:40:27.685
My first job was literally
counting diamonds.

00:40:27.685 --> 00:40:30.853
So on paper, if you were
Apple Computer's, you know,

00:40:30.853 --> 00:40:33.358
recruiter, you said, alright,
so we need someone to go out

00:40:33.358 --> 00:40:36.234
and to convince the most
technical audience we have,

00:40:36.234 --> 00:40:37.825
software developers,

00:40:37.825 --> 00:40:40.652
why they should write Macintosh software.

00:40:40.652 --> 00:40:43.995
I don't think the spec
would say, psych degree,

00:40:43.995 --> 00:40:46.740
law school dropout, marketing MBA,

00:40:46.740 --> 00:40:49.820
currently counting
diamonds in Los Angeles.

00:40:49.820 --> 00:40:50.653
(audience chuckling)

00:40:50.653 --> 00:40:53.961
That might not be the sweet spot, okay?

00:40:53.961 --> 00:40:56.747
But what enabled me to be
successful with Macintosh

00:40:56.747 --> 00:40:58.414
is I love Macintosh.

00:41:00.189 --> 00:41:02.436
The scales were removed from my eyes

00:41:02.436 --> 00:41:04.625
the first time I saw it.

00:41:04.625 --> 00:41:05.889
You know, the clouds parted,

00:41:05.889 --> 00:41:07.786
the angels started playing their trumpets,

00:41:07.786 --> 00:41:10.556
it was a religious experience for me.

00:41:10.556 --> 00:41:12.482
That's why I succeeded
because it ain't because

00:41:12.482 --> 00:41:14.702
of my psych degree and it's not because

00:41:14.702 --> 00:41:16.475
I attended law school for two weeks

00:41:16.475 --> 00:41:19.304
and it's not because I
have a marketing degree

00:41:19.304 --> 00:41:21.140
from UCLA, alright?

00:41:21.140 --> 00:41:22.904
Ignore the irrelevant.

00:41:22.904 --> 00:41:26.197
What is relevant is they love the product.

00:41:26.197 --> 00:41:27.416
Second point.

00:41:27.416 --> 00:41:29.531
You should hire better than yourself.

00:41:29.531 --> 00:41:31.977
Very difficult for most founders,

00:41:31.977 --> 00:41:33.958
particularly engineering types who believe

00:41:33.958 --> 00:41:36.948
that because of their superior chip speed

00:41:36.948 --> 00:41:38.677
that they should be the best salesperson,

00:41:38.677 --> 00:41:40.661
the best finance person,
the best marketing person,

00:41:40.661 --> 00:41:42.841
and the best engineering person.

00:41:42.841 --> 00:41:44.816
You need to hire people better than you.

00:41:44.816 --> 00:41:47.954
A players, by definition, hire A players.

00:41:47.954 --> 00:41:50.478
B players hire C players.

00:41:50.478 --> 00:41:52.739
And C players hire D players.

00:41:52.739 --> 00:41:57.357
So pretty soon you'll be
surrounded by Z players, okay?

00:41:57.357 --> 00:41:59.434
That's what's called the bozo explosion.

00:41:59.434 --> 00:42:00.613
(audience laughing)

00:42:00.613 --> 00:42:04.910
And what follows the bozo
explosion is the layoff, okay?

00:42:04.910 --> 00:42:07.147
So avoid the bozo explosion.

00:42:07.147 --> 00:42:08.399
The third thing is to apply

00:42:08.399 --> 00:42:11.309
the Stanford Shopping Center test.

00:42:11.309 --> 00:42:13.067
The way this works is suppose you were at

00:42:13.067 --> 00:42:14.263
Stanford Shopping Center.

00:42:14.263 --> 00:42:16.465
You see someone who's being
recruited by your company,

00:42:16.465 --> 00:42:18.879
who's interviewing at your company.

00:42:18.879 --> 00:42:20.753
But this is between you and
the person when you first

00:42:20.753 --> 00:42:24.062
see him or her is from me
to the back of the room.

00:42:24.062 --> 00:42:25.982
That person up there has not yet seen me

00:42:25.982 --> 00:42:28.711
but I have seen them, or him, or her.

00:42:28.711 --> 00:42:31.124
So at this point I have sort of choices.

00:42:31.124 --> 00:42:32.934
I can rush over there
and say, hi, how are you?

00:42:32.934 --> 00:42:34.033
I know you're interviewing,

00:42:34.033 --> 00:42:35.961
can I tell you more about the company?

00:42:35.961 --> 00:42:37.781
I really think you
should join our company.

00:42:37.781 --> 00:42:40.184
We're just going to kick some
ass, we're going to make mean,

00:42:40.184 --> 00:42:43.082
and we're going to change the
world with our technology.

00:42:43.082 --> 00:42:46.912
Or, you can say Stanford
Shopping Center isn't that big,

00:42:46.912 --> 00:42:47.983
maybe I'll bump into him.

00:42:47.983 --> 00:42:51.354
If I do, I'll say hello, if
I don't, I won't say hello.

00:42:51.354 --> 00:42:53.393
The third reaction is I'm
going to get in my car

00:42:53.393 --> 00:42:56.356
and go to Vallco Shopping Center, okay?

00:42:56.356 --> 00:42:58.375
If your reaction to the
job candidate is not

00:42:58.375 --> 00:43:01.136
I'm going to run over there
and say hello to the person,

00:43:01.136 --> 00:43:04.158
don't hire the person
because when you're creating

00:43:04.158 --> 00:43:06.306
a start-up, it is a family.

00:43:06.306 --> 00:43:10.219
It is a dysfunctional family,
honestly, but it is a family.

00:43:10.219 --> 00:43:12.618
If you don't feel the
compulsion to run over there

00:43:12.618 --> 00:43:15.197
and say hello, it's probably
not the right person.

